Selectors in Playwright:

    Element Selection:
        This is a concept where it helps us to perform user-like actions, and handle dynamic content loading.

        - CSS Selectors
            - By ID
                - page.click("button#submit")
            - By class
                - page.click(".btn-primary")
            - By attribute
                - page.click("input[name='email']")

        - Text Selectors
            - path.click("text=Submit")

        - XPath Selectors
            - '//tagname[@atrribute = "value"]'
            - It has to be unique
            - It can be found by using 'ctrl + f' after going into the inspect in the browser.
            - Then copy the xpath you created and paste it there and if it highlights the correct one.
            - Then you can consider it as unique.
            - page.click("//button[@id=Submit]")

            - text
                - '//tagname[text()="text"]'
                - '//p[text()="Submit"]'

        - Role Selectors
            - page.get_by_role("button", name="Submit").click()
Basic Actions

        - click
            - page.click("text=Login")

        - type & fill
            - type
                - types slowly, simulating user typing
                - page.type("#username","my_user")
            - fill
                - Directly fills the input
                - page.fill("#password","secret")

        - Select from Dropdown
            - page.select_option("#coutry","India")

        - Check / Uncheck
            - page.check("#terms")
            - page.uncheck("#terms")

        - custom radio check boxes
            - # Click the label instead of the hidden input
            - page.locator("label[for='gender-radio-1']").click()

            - page.get_by_text("Male", exact=True).click()

    - Select_option
        - helps us to select us options if there are options given in inner text
        - page.select_option("select[class*=''] >> nth=0, 'option'")

    - Upload a Single file
        - page.set_input_files("input[type='file']", "Path")

    - Upload Multiple Files
        - page.set_input_files("input[type='file']",[
            "Path",
            "Path"
        ])

    - Clear Uploaded Files
        - page.set_input_files("input[type='file']", [])


Timeouts - Waits
    - Implicit Wait
        - page.set_default_timeout(5000) # 5 seconds

    - Explicit Wait
        - page.wait_for_selector("text=Dashboard")

    - Wait for Navigation
        - with page.expect_navigation():
             page.click("text=Submit")


Assertions:
    - from playwright.sync_api import expect
      expect(page.locator("h1")).to_have_text("Welcome")
      expect(page.locator(#login)).to_be_visible()


DropDowns:
    - # Select by value
        page.select_option("select#state", "NCR")

    - # Select by label (visible text)
        page.select_option("select#state", label="Uttar Pradesh")

    - # Select by index
        page.select_option("select#state", index=2)

    - # Select multiple values (if multi-select dropdown)
        page.select_option("select#subjects", ["Maths", "English"])

    - Custom DropDowns:
        - # Select State
            page.click("#state")              # open dropdown
            page.click("text=Uttar Pradesh")  # choose option

        - # Select City
            page.click("#city")
            page.click("text=Lucknow")

    | ⚡ Rule of Thumb
    |
    | If the dropdown is <select>, always use .select_option().
    | If it’s custom-styled (div, ul, etc.), you need .click() → .click(option).

Note:
    - Best Practice Summary
        - Use Locator API and rely on Playwright’s auto-waiting for most cases.
        - Use waitForSelector for extra control in special situations (page states, non-actionable elements, edge cases).
        - Avoid using hard waits (waitForTimeout) as much as possible—these can degrade reliability.
        - If using waitForSelector, be specific with your selector, state, timeout, and handle possible errors in your test logic.

    - For things like making sure the UI is ready and the proceed with what we want to do.

